Z||000008||bad911||06-18-2001||04:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Neverfinished:<BR><B>Is it worth it to take it out???<BR>I know this sucker weighs like 50 pounds so it would even out the weight dis. but can you notice any difference<BR>thnx<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Depends... if you're a 9/10ths driver autocrossing a well-prepped car, yeah, your stopwatch might see it. If your car's an average daily driver w/ a 100k Iron Duke in it, it's sure not gonna turn you around to beat the Accord that ripped you a new one. The compressor is very close to the center-of-mass of the car, so a "street car" cornering improvement is gonna be zilch and your weight-to-power ratio gain is almost zilch. An '84 compressor [v-belt] weighs 13 lbs, the serpentine belt pulley is probably even lighter. 2700 lb car-- do the math. Bottom line... if you're a gung-ho no-holds-barred kinda guy... then hell yeah, rip that ball-and-chain POS outa there and go buy a new belt.  Otherwise save your time and resources for a bigger mill remembering Rule #1: There's no substitute for cubic inches.<P>The Ark was built and sailed by amateurs...<BR>The Titanic was built and sailed by professionals.   <P><P>------------------<BR>87 2.8 AT SE<BR>85 WannaCaddie<BR>84 Rolling Stock||||reg||1
